A||||5||PAUL88GT||Need help in solving backfiring problem||||1||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||03-02-2001||12:04 PM||||I bought an 86SE V6 about a month ago.  I am having trouble getting it to run properly and I need help from the forum. <P>The car sat for at least a year before I bought it.  It will start, but then it runs for a few minutes at high idle, then backfires very loud and dies.  The backfiring has actually busted the muffler.  It will not respond very much to the throttle. Exhaust smells rich.<P>Here is what I have done so far:<P>New plugs and plug wires<BR>New distributor cap and rotor button<BR>Drained old gas and put on new fuel filter<BR>Set the timing to 10 degree base timing using average of #1 and #4<BR>New alternator (bearing was bad on original one)<BR>New ignition module<BR>No obvious vacuum leaks, but I have not put a gauge on the system<BR>Added injector cleaner to fuel<BR>Replaced coil with one from running car and still has same backfiring problem<P>My friend suspects that the injectors areclogged from sitting for so long, but he is only a shade tree mechanic.  I want to get feedback from the forum before I pull the injectors.<P>I also have to replace the bent clutch pedal and bleed the clutch hydraulics, so I have not had it on the road yet.<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Paul <P><BR>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 03-02-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 03-02-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||03-02-2001||02:01 PM||||i dont have a v6 but heres a few suggestions.<P>if its running rich, then it might not be a fuel problem.<P>Are you sure you put the wires to the correct cylinders and on the dist cap.<P>The previous owner could have put them in wrong and you were following what he had.<P>See what the firing order is, and get it to TDC.  Look at the dist cap and see if the rotor is pointing to the post of the wire going to the 1st cylinder and go from there.<P>I would loosen the distr and redo the timing, and do it by sound and not what the timing light says.  Just to see if it stops backfiring.<P>Is your pcv valve clean, it should rattle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||03-02-2001||02:03 PM||||Did you check the cat.?  When they get clogged they can cause backfiring (at least mine did).  And they can pretty much screw up everything.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-02-2001||02:17 PM||||First, Clean out IAC. Note: It may idle high. The car needs to be driven<BR>above 40mph to reset it. Next I would change the oxygen sensor. This<BR>controls air/fuel mixture. It sounds like the car is running way too rich<BR>and the unburnt fuel ignites in the exhaust.<BR>Finally, you could short the ECM connector (like you did when you timed it)<BR>and run it. If it still runs rich you may have a bad injector. With the<BR>connector shorted the ECM ignores inputs from the sensors and uses a default<BR>mode.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||03-02-2001||11:40 PM||||All,<BR>I have changed the oxygen sensor. I know the plug wires are correct because it runs strong for about 30 seconds before backfiring and shutting down.<BR>I will try shorting the ECM and see how it runs.<BR>I will also clean the IAC valve.<P>Do I have to remove the cat to determine if it is clogged?<BR>Paul<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||PAUL88GT||03-04-2001||10:45 PM||||Update:<P>Rebuilt the distributor (new ignition module, new pick-up coil, good cleaning).  Put a temporary patch on cracked EGR tube. It is purring like a kitten now. Runs great.<P>Looks like the $300 I paid for the car is a good deal.<P>Still have to change the clutch pedal before I can take the first test drive.<P>All in all, it was a productive Sunday.||||reg||1
